Biological Concepts Behind BioInform2

This document explains the biological concepts behind the sequencing pipeline used in BioInform2.

DNA Basics

DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid) contains genetic information for all living organisms.

DNA is composed of four nucleotides:

A – Adenine
T – Thymine
C – Cytosine
G – Guanine

Base pairing rules:

A pairs with T
C pairs with G

The sequence of these nucleotides encodes biological instructions.

Next Generation Sequencing

Next Generation Sequencing is a technology that allows millions of DNA fragments to be sequenced simultaneously.

NGS technologies generate massive volumes of genomic data that must be processed using bioinformatics pipelines.

Sequencing machines produce output data in the FASTQ format.

FASTQ Data

FASTQ files store sequencing reads along with quality scores.

Example structure:

@SEQ_ID
GATTTGGGGTTCAAAGCAGTATCGATCAAATAGTAAAT
+
!''*((((***+))%%%++)(%%%%).1***-+*''))**

The quality scores indicate confidence in each base call.

Sequence Alignment

Sequence alignment maps DNA reads to a reference genome.

This step determines where each DNA fragment originates from within the genome.

The alignment tool used in this pipeline is BWA.

SAM File

The output of alignment is stored in the SAM (Sequence Alignment Map) format.

SAM files contain:

read name

mapping position

alignment quality

sequence information

SAM files are typically converted to BAM format for efficient storage and analysis.

Role of Bioinformatics Pipelines

Bioinformatics pipelines automate genomic analysis by connecting multiple tools into a single workflow.

These pipelines are essential for:

genomic research

disease mutation discovery

cancer genomics

personalized medicine



